  # ── Kubernetes Fundamentals kcna-241–254 ─────────────────────────────────
  {"id":"kcna-241","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is the primary purpose of an init container in a Pod?","options":{"A":"To run alongside the main container and provide logging functionality","B":"To perform initialization tasks that must complete before app containers start","C":"To restart the main container if it fails","D":"To expose metrics from the main container"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: Init containers run and complete before any app containers start, making them ideal for setup tasks such as populating shared volumes, waiting for dependencies, or registering with external services. A: That describes a sidecar container, not an init container. C: Init containers do not restart failed main containers; that is handled by restartPolicy. D: Exposing metrics is a role for an adapter or sidecar, not an init container.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-242","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"easy","question":"In a kubeconfig file, what does a \"context\" bind together?","options":{"A":"A namespace and a set of labels","B":"A cluster, a user, and optionally a namespace","C":"A cluster and a set of RBAC roles","D":"A user and a set of resource quotas"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: A kubeconfig context is a named tuple that references a cluster entry, a user entry, and an optional namespace, allowing kubectl to know where to connect and as whom. A: Labels are not part of a context. C: RBAC roles are not stored in kubeconfig. D: Resource quotas are cluster-side objects, not kubeconfig entries.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/organize-cluster-access-kubeconfig/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-243","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which kubectl command switches the active context in your kubeconfig?","options":{"A":"kubectl config set-context","B":"kubectl config use-context","C":"kubectl config get-contexts","D":"kubectl context switch"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: kubectl config use-context <name> updates the current-context field in the kubeconfig file so subsequent commands target the named context. A: set-context creates or modifies a context definition but does not make it active. C: get-contexts lists available contexts without changing the active one. D: This is not a valid kubectl subcommand.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_config/kubectl_config_use-context/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-244","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"easy","question":"A Pod has no resource requests or limits set on any of its containers. Which QoS class is assigned to it?","options":{"A":"Guaranteed","B":"Burstable","C":"BestEffort","D":"Default"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: When none of the containers in a Pod specify CPU or memory requests or limits, Kubernetes assigns the BestEffort QoS class, making it the first candidate for eviction under resource pressure. A: Guaranteed requires every container to have equal requests and limits for both CPU and memory. B: Burstable requires at least one container to have a request or limit but not the full Guaranteed criteria. D: Default is not a Kubernetes QoS class.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-qos/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-245","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is the role of the \"ambassador\" multi-container pattern?","options":{"A":"Transform or reformat the main container output before it is consumed","B":"Proxy network traffic between the main container and external services","C":"Collect logs from the main container and forward them to a central store","D":"Provide shared storage initialization before the main container starts"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: The ambassador pattern places a proxy container in the Pod that intercepts and manages outbound or inbound network calls on behalf of the main container, abstracting external service endpoints. A: Reformatting output describes the adapter pattern. C: Log forwarding is a sidecar responsibility. D: Storage initialization before startup describes an init container.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/#workload-resources-for-managing-pods","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-246","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"medium","question":"Init containers are listed under spec.initContainers. In what order does Kubernetes run them?","options":{"A":"All init containers run in parallel, then app containers start","B":"They run sequentially in the order listed; each must complete successfully before the next begins","C":"They run in reverse order of declaration to resolve dependencies","D":"The scheduler picks the order based on resource availability"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: Kubernetes runs init containers one at a time in the exact order they appear in spec.initContainers. Each init container must exit with code 0 before the next one starts. A: Parallel execution does not apply to init containers. C: Reverse ordering is not how Kubernetes handles init containers. D: The scheduler assigns a node but does not control init container ordering.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers/#understanding-init-containers","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-247","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"medium","question":"A Pod has a container with cpu request=500m, cpu limit=500m, memory request=256Mi, memory limit=256Mi. What QoS class is assigned?","options":{"A":"BestEffort","B":"Burstable","C":"Guaranteed","D":"Critical"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: Guaranteed QoS is assigned when every container in the Pod has CPU and memory requests set equal to their respective limits. The described container satisfies this condition. A: BestEffort requires no requests or limits at all. B: Burstable applies when requests exist but are not equal to limits, or only some containers have requests set. D: Critical is not a Kubernetes QoS class.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/pod-qos/#guaranteed","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-248","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which node condition indicates that the node is running low on available process IDs?","options":{"A":"MemoryPressure","B":"DiskPressure","C":"PIDPressure","D":"NetworkUnavailable"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: The PIDPressure condition is set to True when the node detects that the number of available process IDs is approaching the configured limit, which can prevent new processes from being created. A: MemoryPressure reflects low available memory. B: DiskPressure reflects low available disk space or inodes. D: NetworkUnavailable indicates the network is not correctly configured for the node.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/nodes/#condition","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-249","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"medium","question":"How does an EndpointSlice differ from a classic Endpoints object?","options":{"A":"EndpointSlices store TLS certificates for each endpoint","B":"EndpointSlices split endpoint data across multiple objects, improving scalability for large Services","C":"EndpointSlices replace Ingress objects for HTTP routing","D":"EndpointSlices are only used with headless Services"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: EndpointSlices shard endpoint information into smaller objects (default max 100 endpoints each), reducing the size of updates sent to watchers and improving control-plane scalability compared to a single Endpoints object. A: TLS certificates are not stored in EndpointSlices. C: EndpointSlices have nothing to do with Ingress or HTTP routing. D: EndpointSlices are used for all Service types, not only headless ones.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/endpoint-slices/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-250","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"medium","question":"In the admission control pipeline, which webhook type runs first — Mutating or Validating?","options":{"A":"Validating webhooks run first, then Mutating webhooks apply changes","B":"Both types run simultaneously in parallel","C":"Mutating webhooks run first, then Validating webhooks inspect the final object","D":"The order depends on the webhook failurePolicy setting"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: Kubernetes always runs MutatingAdmissionWebhook before ValidatingAdmissionWebhook. This ensures that validating webhooks see the fully mutated object rather than an intermediate state. A: This is incorrect; validation comes after mutation. B: The two phases are sequential, not parallel. D: failurePolicy controls what happens when a webhook fails to respond, not the execution order.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/extensible-admission-controllers/#what-are-admission-webhooks","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-251","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"medium","question":"What does the `kubectl diff` command do?","options":{"A":"Shows differences between two Kubernetes cluster versions","B":"Compares local manifest files against the live state in the cluster without applying changes","C":"Displays the git diff of files in the current directory","D":"Lists API resources that differ between namespaces"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: kubectl diff sends the supplied manifests to the server-side dry-run endpoint and displays a unified diff showing what would change if the manifests were applied, allowing operators to preview changes safely. A: Cluster version comparison is done with other tools, not kubectl diff. C: kubectl has no git integration. D: Comparing resources across namespaces is not what kubectl diff does.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_diff/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-252","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"hard","question":"A PriorityClass is created with preemptionPolicy: Never. What does this mean for Pods that use it?","options":{"A":"Pods with this class are never scheduled on nodes that already have running Pods","B":"Pods with this class have higher scheduling priority but will not evict lower-priority Pods to free resources","C":"Pods with this class are immediately terminated if a higher-priority Pod needs their node","D":"Pods with this class ignore all resource quotas"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: preemptionPolicy: Never means the Pod benefits from a higher priority value for scheduling queue ordering, but the scheduler will not preempt (evict) lower-priority Pods to make room; instead the Pod waits in the queue until natural capacity becomes available. A: PriorityClass does not restrict which nodes Pods can use. C: That behavior describes a Pod being preempted, the opposite of Never. D: PriorityClass has no effect on ResourceQuota enforcement.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/pod-priority-preemption/#non-preempting-priority-class","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-253","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"hard","question":"Pod overhead is defined in a RuntimeClass. How does it affect Pod scheduling and resource accounting?","options":{"A":"Overhead is subtracted from node allocatable capacity to make more room for Pods","B":"Overhead values are added to the sum of container resource requests when computing the Pod's total resource footprint for scheduling and quota purposes","C":"Overhead only affects cgroup limits, not scheduler decisions","D":"Overhead is applied only when the Pod exceeds its resource limits"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: When a RuntimeClass specifies overhead, the kubelet adds those CPU and memory values to the sum of all container requests and limits. The scheduler uses this inflated total to determine node fit, and ResourceQuota counts it toward namespace consumption, accurately accounting for the sandbox cost of virtualization runtimes like Kata Containers. A: Overhead is added to Pod cost, not subtracted from node capacity directly. C: The scheduler absolutely factors overhead into its feasibility checks. D: Overhead is always applied regardless of whether limits are exceeded.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/pod-overhead/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-254","domain":"Kubernetes Fundamentals","difficulty":"hard","question":"According to the Kubernetes API deprecation policy, how long must a deprecated API version remain available after it is deprecated?","options":{"A":"It can be removed in the very next release","B":"It must remain available for at least 1 release for alpha, 3 releases for beta, and 12 months or 3 releases (whichever is longer) for GA APIs","C":"All deprecated APIs must remain for exactly 2 releases regardless of stability level","D":"Deprecated APIs are never removed; they are only hidden from documentation"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: The Kubernetes API deprecation policy differentiates by stability level. Alpha API versions may be removed after one release. Beta API versions must remain available for at least 3 releases. GA (stable) API versions must remain for at least 12 months or 3 releases, whichever is longer. A: Immediate removal applies only to alpha versions, and even then only after one release, not the very next. C: A single flat 2-release rule does not match the policy. D: Kubernetes does remove deprecated APIs on schedule.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/using-api/deprecation-policy/","llm_validated":0},

  # ── Container Orchestration kcna-255–258 ──────────────────────────────────
  {"id":"kcna-255","domain":"Container Orchestration","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is the primary role of the Container Network Interface (CNI) in Kubernetes?","options":{"A":"To manage container image pulls from registries","B":"To provide a standard plugin interface for configuring network connectivity for containers","C":"To encrypt traffic between the API server and kubelets","D":"To assign persistent IP addresses to nodes in the cluster"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: CNI defines a standard interface between container runtimes and network plugins, allowing plugins to set up and tear down network interfaces for containers. A: Image pulls are handled by the container runtime, not CNI. C: API server-to-kubelet encryption uses TLS certificates, not CNI. D: Node IP assignment is handled by the cloud provider or static configuration, not CNI.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/compute-storage-net/network-plugins/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-256","domain":"Container Orchestration","difficulty":"medium","question":"A namespace has the label `pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted` applied. Which of the following Pod configurations would be REJECTED by the Pod Security Admission controller?","options":{"A":"A Pod with securityContext.runAsNonRoot: true and no privileged containers","B":"A Pod that drops all capabilities and sets allowPrivilegeEscalation: false","C":"A Pod with a container running as root (runAsUser: 0) and no securityContext restrictions","D":"A Pod with a read-only root filesystem and seccompProfile type RuntimeDefault"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: The 'restricted' Pod Security Standard requires Pods to run as non-root, drop all capabilities, disallow privilege escalation, and use a seccomp profile. A Pod running as root violates the restricted policy and will be rejected. A: Running as non-root with no privileged containers satisfies restricted requirements. B: Dropping all capabilities and disabling privilege escalation meets restricted policy requirements. D: A read-only root filesystem with a RuntimeDefault seccomp profile complies with the restricted standard.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-257","domain":"Container Orchestration","difficulty":"medium","question":"A Pod cannot reach a Service by its DNS name inside the cluster. Which sequence of checks is the most appropriate initial troubleshooting approach?","options":{"A":"Restart the kube-proxy DaemonSet, then recreate the Service","B":"Verify the CoreDNS pods are running, confirm the Service selector matches the Pod labels, then check for NetworkPolicies that may block traffic","C":"Delete and recreate the Pod, then inspect kubelet logs on the node","D":"Check the container image tag, then review the node's firewall rules"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: DNS resolution failures require checking CoreDNS health first; if DNS resolves but traffic fails, the Service selector must match target Pod labels; NetworkPolicies can silently block traffic even when DNS and selectors are correct. This order covers the three most common root causes systematically. A: Restarting kube-proxy without diagnosis is disruptive and skips DNS and selector checks. C: Recreating the Pod does not address DNS or network configuration issues. D: The container image tag and node firewall rules are unlikely causes for intra-cluster Service DNS failures.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/debug-service/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-258","domain":"Container Orchestration","difficulty":"hard","question":"An administrator wants to use a proprietary SAN storage system with Kubernetes without modifying the Kubernetes core code. After deploying the vendor's CSI driver, which Kubernetes objects must be created so that a Pod can consume a volume from that storage system?","options":{"A":"A ConfigMap referencing the storage endpoint, and a VolumeMount in the Pod spec","B":"A StorageClass that references the CSI driver's provisioner name, a PersistentVolumeClaim bound to that StorageClass, and a Pod spec that references the PVC","C":"A CSINode object created manually by the administrator, and a HostPath volume in the Pod spec","D":"A DaemonSet running the storage client binary on every node, and a PersistentVolume with hostPath type"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: CSI enables pluggable storage by having the vendor supply a driver deployed as Pods in the cluster. A StorageClass is created with the CSI driver's provisioner identifier; a PVC referencing that StorageClass triggers dynamic provisioning via the CSI driver; the Pod references the PVC to consume the volume. This is the standard CSI consumption workflow. A: A ConfigMap alone cannot provision or attach storage; VolumeMounts reference volumes already defined in the Pod spec. C: CSINode objects are created automatically by the CSI node driver, not manually; HostPath does not use the CSI driver. D: The CSI driver itself runs as a DaemonSet/Deployment deployed by the vendor; a HostPath PersistentVolume bypasses CSI entirely.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/volumes/#csi","llm_validated":0},

  # ── Cloud Native Application Delivery kcna-259–276 ───────────────────────
  {"id":"kcna-259","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is a Helm Chart?","options":{"A":"A running instance of a Helm package deployed to a cluster","B":"A collection of files that describe a related set of Kubernetes resources","C":"A versioned snapshot of a Helm repository index","D":"A Kubernetes CRD that Helm uses to track deployments"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: A Helm Chart is a collection of files that describe a related set of Kubernetes resources. It packages templates, default values, and metadata together so applications can be deployed consistently. A: That describes a Helm release, not a chart. C: That describes a repository index entry. D: Helm does not use a CRD to track deployments; it uses Secrets or ConfigMaps for release state.","doc_link":"https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-260","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which file in a Helm Chart contains the chart's name, version, and description metadata?","options":{"A":"values.yaml","B":"templates/NOTES.txt","C":"Chart.yaml","D":"requirements.yaml"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: Chart.yaml is the mandatory metadata file in a Helm Chart that contains fields such as name, version, appVersion, and description. A: values.yaml holds default configuration values for templates, not chart metadata. B: NOTES.txt is an optional file that displays post-install instructions. D: requirements.yaml was used in Helm 2 for dependencies; Helm 3 uses the dependencies field inside Chart.yaml.","doc_link":"https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/#the-chartyaml-file","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-261","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"easy","question":"What does the `helm rollback` command do?","options":{"A":"Deletes the current release and reinstalls the previous chart version from the repository","B":"Reverts a release to a previously deployed revision stored in release history","C":"Restores the values.yaml file to its default state","D":"Undoes any manual kubectl edits made to resources managed by Helm"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: `helm rollback <release> <revision>` reverts a named release to a specific previous revision stored in Helm's release history, redeploying the manifests from that revision. A: Helm does not delete and reinstall; it applies the stored revision in-place. C: values.yaml on disk is not modified by rollback. D: Helm tracks its own release history, not out-of-band kubectl changes.","doc_link":"https://helm.sh/docs/helm/helm_rollback/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-262","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"easy","question":"In GitOps, what is the single source of truth for the desired state of a system?","options":{"A":"The live state of resources running in the Kubernetes cluster","B":"The container image registry","C":"A Git repository containing declarative configuration","D":"The CI pipeline artifact store"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: The core GitOps principle is that a Git repository holds the declarative desired state of the system. The GitOps operator continuously reconciles the cluster toward that state. A: The live cluster state is what GitOps reconciles toward the Git state, not the source of truth. B: Image registries store artifacts, but cluster configuration lives in Git. D: CI artifact stores are part of the build pipeline, not the desired-state definition.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/manage-deployment/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-263","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"medium","question":"A Helm release is at revision 5. A team runs `helm upgrade` which succeeds, making revision 6. They then discover a regression and run `helm rollback myapp 4`. What revision number will the release be at after the rollback?","options":{"A":"4","B":"5","C":"6","D":"7"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Answer D: Helm rollback does not go back in history; it creates a new revision. Rolling back to revision 4 applies revision 4's manifests but stores the result as revision 7. This preserves a complete, linear audit trail. A: Revision 4 is the target content, not the resulting revision number. B: Revision 5 is an earlier revision in history. C: Revision 6 is the failed upgrade that was rolled back from.","doc_link":"https://helm.sh/docs/helm/helm_rollback/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-264","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"medium","question":"A Helm hook is annotated with `helm.sh/hook: pre-install`. When does this hook execute?","options":{"A":"After all resources in the chart are created but before the release is marked successful","B":"Before any resources in the chart are rendered and applied to the cluster","C":"After the chart is rendered but before any chart resources are installed","D":"During every subsequent `helm upgrade` before new resources are applied"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: A pre-install hook runs after Helm renders the chart templates but before any of the chart's main resources are created in the cluster. This is useful for tasks like database migrations or secret creation that must complete before the application starts. A: That describes a post-install hook. B: Hooks are rendered from templates, so rendering always happens first. D: pre-install runs only on initial install, not on upgrades; pre-upgrade would apply on upgrades.","doc_link":"https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts_hooks/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-265","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"medium","question":"Which CI/CD pipeline stage is responsible for publishing a built container image to a registry so later stages can reference it?","options":{"A":"Build","B":"Test","C":"Push","D":"Deploy"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: The Push stage uploads the built and tagged container image to a container registry (such as Docker Hub or ECR) so that subsequent deploy stages can pull and run it. A: The Build stage compiles code and creates the image locally, but does not publish it. B: The Test stage runs unit, integration, or security tests against the built artifact. D: The Deploy stage retrieves the already-pushed image from the registry and applies it to an environment.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/images/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-266","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"medium","question":"In ArgoCD, what is the difference between a Sync operation and a Refresh operation?","options":{"A":"Refresh fetches the latest Git state and updates the diff; Sync applies the Git state to the cluster","B":"Sync fetches the latest Git state; Refresh applies it to the cluster","C":"Refresh restarts all pods in the application; Sync reconciles only CRDs","D":"They are aliases for the same operation in ArgoCD"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Answer A: In ArgoCD, Refresh re-fetches the desired state from Git (or Helm) and recomputes the diff between desired and live state, but does not modify the cluster. Sync then applies the desired state to the cluster, creating, updating, or deleting resources as needed. B: This reverses the correct definitions. C: Refresh does not restart pods. D: They are distinct operations with different effects.","doc_link":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/core_concepts/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-267","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"medium","question":"What does enabling `selfHeal: true` in an ArgoCD Application spec cause ArgoCD to do?","options":{"A":"Automatically restart pods that fail their liveness probe","B":"Automatically sync the cluster back to the Git desired state when live resources drift","C":"Automatically create a Git commit reverting manual changes","D":"Automatically upgrade the ArgoCD controller when a new version is available"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: When selfHeal is enabled, ArgoCD detects drift between the live cluster state and the desired state in Git and automatically triggers a sync to reconcile the difference, without requiring a human to initiate it. A: Pod restarts due to liveness probes are handled by the Kubernetes kubelet, not ArgoCD. C: ArgoCD modifies the cluster to match Git; it does not push changes back to Git. D: ArgoCD version upgrades are unrelated to the selfHeal setting.","doc_link":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/auto_sync/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-268","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"medium","question":"In Flux, what does a `GitRepository` source object define?","options":{"A":"A Kubernetes Deployment that pulls and runs code directly from a Git repo","B":"A reference to a Git repository, branch, and polling interval that Flux uses to fetch manifests","C":"An ArgoCD Application CRD converted to Flux format","D":"A secret containing Git credentials for kubectl to use during apply"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: A Flux GitRepository source CRD specifies the Git repository URL, branch or tag reference, and the interval at which Flux should poll for changes. Other Flux objects such as Kustomization or HelmRelease reference this source to obtain their configuration. A: Flux does not run code directly from Git; it applies Kubernetes manifests. C: GitRepository is a Flux-native concept, not a converted ArgoCD object. D: Git credentials are stored in a separate Secret referenced by the GitRepository, not defined within it.","doc_link":"https://fluxcd.io/flux/components/source/gitrepositories/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-269","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"medium","question":"A team wants to promote a container image from staging to production. Which image promotion pattern involves updating the production GitOps repository with the image tag that passed staging tests?","options":{"A":"Blue-green promotion","B":"Canary gating","C":"Environment-branch promotion","D":"Tag-based GitOps promotion"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Answer D: Tag-based GitOps promotion involves a CI/CD pipeline updating the production environment's Git repository (or a specific file such as values.yaml) with the exact image tag that successfully completed staging validation. The GitOps operator then reconciles production to use that tag. A: Blue-green is a traffic-switching deployment strategy, not a promotion pattern. B: Canary gating is a progressive traffic-shifting technique. C: Environment-branch promotion uses separate Git branches per environment but does not specifically describe image-tag updating.","doc_link":"https://fluxcd.io/flux/guides/image-update/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-270","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"medium","question":"In a blue-green deployment, what triggers the actual traffic switch from the old (blue) version to the new (green) version?","options":{"A":"The green Deployment's Pod count reaching the desired replica count","B":"A Kubernetes Service selector update that points to the green Pods","C":"Termination of all blue Pods by the cluster autoscaler","D":"The green Pods passing their liveness probe at least once"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: In a blue-green deployment, both the blue and green environments run simultaneously. The traffic switch is performed by updating the Kubernetes Service's label selector to target the green Pods instead of the blue Pods, instantly redirecting all incoming traffic. A: Reaching the desired replica count is a prerequisite but does not itself switch traffic. C: Blue Pods are usually kept running until after the switch to allow instant rollback. D: Liveness probes keep pods running but do not redirect Service traffic.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/#canary-deployment","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-271","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"medium","question":"In a canary deployment, 10% of traffic is routed to the canary version. What determines that percentage in a standard Kubernetes-only approach (without a service mesh)?","options":{"A":"The weight field in the Kubernetes Ingress resource","B":"The ratio of canary Pod replicas to total Pod replicas across both versions","C":"A TrafficSplit CRD applied to the cluster","D":"The canary annotation on the Deployment resource"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: Without a service mesh or advanced ingress controller, Kubernetes distributes traffic roughly proportionally to the number of healthy Pod endpoints behind a Service. Running 1 canary Pod alongside 9 stable Pods approximates 10% canary traffic. A: Standard Kubernetes Ingress does not support traffic weight splitting natively. C: TrafficSplit is a Service Mesh Interface (SMI) CRD and requires a service mesh, so it is not a standard Kubernetes-only mechanism. D: There is no native Kubernetes Deployment annotation for canary traffic splitting.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/#canary-deployment","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-272","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"medium","question":"What is the primary reason to use a multi-stage Docker build in a CI/CD pipeline?","options":{"A":"To allow multiple teams to build the same image simultaneously","B":"To reduce the final image size by excluding build tools and intermediate artifacts","C":"To enable caching of base images across different registries","D":"To enforce that unit tests run before the image is tagged"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: Multi-stage builds use one stage (e.g., a compiler image) to build the application and a subsequent minimal stage (e.g., a distroless or alpine image) that copies only the compiled binary. Build tools, source code, and intermediate files are discarded, significantly reducing the final image size and attack surface. A: Multi-stage builds are a single-Dockerfile concept and do not address concurrent team builds. C: Image caching is a separate concern handled by build cache mechanisms, not multi-stage builds specifically. D: Although tests can be included as a stage, the primary purpose is image size reduction.","doc_link":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-273","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"hard","question":"Why is tagging a container image with `latest` considered risky in a continuous delivery pipeline?","options":{"A":"The `latest` tag is rejected by most container registries in production namespaces","B":"Kubernetes does not support pulling images tagged `latest` in production clusters","C":"The `latest` tag is mutable and can be overwritten, making it impossible to guarantee which image version is actually deployed","D":"Using `latest` disables Kubernetes image pull caching, causing slower pod startup"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: The `latest` tag is a mutable pointer that any push can overwrite. In a CD pipeline this means different environments or rollbacks may inadvertently run different image versions while appearing to reference the same tag, destroying reproducibility and auditability. Immutable, content-addressed tags (e.g., a git SHA or semantic version) are preferred. A: Registries do not block the latest tag; some policies may discourage it, but it is not rejected universally. B: Kubernetes can pull `latest`-tagged images; it is not blocked. D: Kubernetes imagePullPolicy controls caching behavior regardless of the tag name.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/images/#image-names","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-274","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"hard","question":"A Flux HelmRelease has `interval: 5m` set. What does this interval control?","options":{"A":"How often Flux checks for new Helm chart versions in the chart repository","B":"How often the Flux helm-controller reconciles the HelmRelease against its desired state","C":"The maximum time allowed for a Helm upgrade to complete before it is rolled back","D":"How often Flux purges old Helm release history revisions"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: The interval field on a Flux HelmRelease tells the helm-controller how frequently to reconcile the release — comparing the live Helm release state to the desired state defined in the HelmRelease spec and applying corrections if they differ. A: Chart repository polling is controlled by the HelmRepository source object's interval, not the HelmRelease interval. C: Upgrade timeout is controlled by a separate timeout or install.timeout field. D: History pruning is not controlled by the reconciliation interval.","doc_link":"https://fluxcd.io/flux/components/helm/helmreleases/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-275","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"hard","question":"What does the OCI (Open Container Initiative) Image Specification define?","options":{"A":"A standard API for container orchestration across different runtime environments","B":"The format of a container image manifest, layers, and configuration so images are portable across runtimes","C":"A security scanning protocol that registries must implement to certify images","D":"The network interface contract between a container runtime and the Kubernetes kubelet"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: The OCI Image Specification defines the on-disk format of a container image, including the image manifest (listing layers), the image index (for multi-platform images), individual layer tarballs, and the image configuration JSON. This ensures images built by one tool can be run by any OCI-compliant runtime. A: Container orchestration APIs are outside OCI scope; that is closer to Kubernetes or the CRI. C: Security scanning is not part of the OCI Image Specification. D: The CRI (Container Runtime Interface) defines the kubelet-to-runtime contract, not OCI.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/containers/#container-images","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-276","domain":"Cloud Native Application Delivery","difficulty":"hard","question":"A team uses Kustomize and needs to change a single field deep in a resource that is shared across many overlays. They want to avoid duplicating the entire resource. Which Kustomize patch type is best suited when the change targets a specific path using a JSON Pointer?","options":{"A":"Strategic merge patch","B":"JSON 6902 patch","C":"configMapGenerator","D":"commonLabels overlay"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: A JSON 6902 patch (RFC 6902) uses explicit operations (add, remove, replace, move, copy, test) with JSON Pointer paths to precisely target a single field in a resource without reproducing the surrounding structure. This is ideal for pinpoint changes in shared base resources. A: A strategic merge patch requires reproducing the resource's identifying fields and the section containing the change, which is more verbose and less precise for single-field edits. C: configMapGenerator creates ConfigMap resources and is unrelated to patching existing resources. D: commonLabels adds labels to all resources globally and cannot target a specific field at a specific path.","doc_link":"https://kubectl.docs.kubernetes.io/references/kustomize/kustomization/patches/","llm_validated":0},

  # ── Cloud Native Architecture kcna-277–300 ────────────────────────────────
  {"id":"kcna-277","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"easy","question":"Which Prometheus metric type always increases monotonically and is reset to zero only when the process restarts?","options":{"A":"Gauge","B":"Counter","C":"Histogram","D":"Summary"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: A Counter is a cumulative metric that only ever increases (or resets to zero on process restart). It is used for things like total HTTP requests or errors. A: A Gauge can go up or down, representing a current value like memory usage. C: A Histogram samples observations and counts them in configurable buckets. D: A Summary also samples observations but calculates configurable quantiles over a sliding time window.","doc_link":"https://prometheus.io/docs/concepts/metric_types/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-278","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"Prometheus uses a pull-based model to collect metrics. What does this mean in practice?","options":{"A":"Prometheus agents push metrics to a central server over gRPC","B":"Prometheus scrapes HTTP endpoints exposed by targets at a configured scrape_interval","C":"Applications call the Prometheus API to register their metric streams","D":"Metrics are written directly to Prometheus storage by a sidecar agent"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: In the pull model, Prometheus periodically sends HTTP GET requests to /metrics endpoints on configured targets according to scrape_interval. This design keeps the collection logic centralised and simplifies firewall rules in many environments. A: That describes a push model (e.g., Pushgateway or remote-write). C: Prometheus has no such registration API. D: While sidecars exist, Prometheus itself initiates the scrape.","doc_link":"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-279","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"In PromQL, what does the function rate(http_requests_total[5m]) compute?","options":{"A":"The total number of HTTP requests in the last 5 minutes","B":"The per-second average rate of increase of the counter over the last 5 minutes","C":"The maximum value of the counter observed during the last 5 minutes","D":"The difference between the latest and earliest counter values in the range"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: rate() calculates the per-second average rate of increase of a counter over the specified range window, automatically accounting for counter resets. A: increase() (not rate()) returns the total increase over the window; rate() normalises this to a per-second figure. C: max_over_time() would return the maximum. D: That describes a partial behaviour of increase() but without the reset handling that rate() applies.","doc_link":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/querying/functions/#rate","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-280","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"hard","question":"Prometheus Alertmanager receives firing alerts from Prometheus servers. Which Alertmanager feature prevents a flood of redundant notifications when a parent alert is already firing?","options":{"A":"Silences","B":"Grouping","C":"Inhibition","D":"Routing trees"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: Inhibition allows Alertmanager to suppress notifications for a set of alerts (the inhibited set) when another specified alert (the inhibiting alert) is already firing. For example, a cluster-down alert can inhibit all node-level alerts from that cluster. A: Silences mute all alerts matching a set of matchers for a time window, typically used for planned maintenance. B: Grouping batches related alerts into a single notification to reduce noise. D: Routing trees determine which receiver handles which alert but do not themselves suppress redundant alerts.","doc_link":"https://prometheus.io/docs/alerting/latest/alertmanager/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-281","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"In distributed tracing, what is the relationship between a trace and a span?","options":{"A":"A span is a complete end-to-end request journey; a trace is a single timed operation within it","B":"A trace is the complete end-to-end request journey composed of one or more spans, each representing a single timed operation","C":"A trace and a span are synonymous terms used interchangeably by OpenTelemetry","D":"A span aggregates multiple traces to produce a latency histogram"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: A trace represents the full lifecycle of a request as it travels through a distributed system. It is composed of spans, where each span represents a single named, timed operation (e.g., an HTTP call, a database query). Spans carry a trace ID to link them together and may have parent-child relationships. A: This reverses the correct definitions. C: They are distinct concepts with different purposes. D: Spans do not aggregate traces; it is the other way around.","doc_link":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/signals/traces/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-282","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"OpenTelemetry defines OTLP as its native wire protocol. What does OTLP stand for and what signals does it carry?","options":{"A":"OpenTelemetry Transport Layer Protocol; carries only traces","B":"OpenTelemetry Line Protocol; carries metrics and logs but not traces","C":"OpenTelemetry Protocol; carries traces, metrics, and logs over gRPC or HTTP/JSON","D":"Open Tracing and Logging Protocol; carries traces and logs over UDP"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: OTLP (OpenTelemetry Protocol) is the vendor-neutral data format and transport protocol defined by OpenTelemetry. It carries all three observability signals — traces, metrics, and logs — and is transported over gRPC or HTTP with JSON or Protobuf encoding. A: OTLP carries all three signals, not only traces. B: The name is wrong and logs-only is incorrect. D: OTLP uses gRPC or HTTP, not UDP, and its name and scope are misrepresented.","doc_link":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otlp/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-283","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"easy","question":"How does Grafana Loki differ from Prometheus in its approach to storing observability data?","options":{"A":"Loki stores structured metrics time series; Prometheus stores raw log lines","B":"Loki indexes only log metadata (labels) and stores log lines compressed; Prometheus stores numeric metric samples","C":"Loki and Prometheus both store metrics but Loki adds anomaly detection","D":"Loki requires a Kafka broker to ingest logs; Prometheus requires Kafka for metrics"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: Loki is a log aggregation system inspired by Prometheus. Unlike traditional log systems that index the full log content, Loki indexes only the label metadata and stores the log lines in compressed chunks. This makes it cost-efficient. Prometheus stores time-series numeric metric samples, not logs. A: This reverses the roles of the two systems. C: Loki handles logs, not metrics, and anomaly detection is not a core feature. D: Neither system requires Kafka; Loki uses Promtail or OTLP for log ingestion.","doc_link":"https://grafana.com/docs/loki/latest/get-started/overview/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-284","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"easy","question":"What are the three pillars of observability in cloud-native systems?","options":{"A":"Metrics, Alerts, and Dashboards","B":"Logs, Traces, and Profiles","C":"Metrics, Logs, and Traces","D":"Spans, Events, and Counters"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: The three pillars of observability are Metrics (numeric measurements over time, e.g., request rate), Logs (timestamped records of discrete events), and Traces (records of request flows through distributed systems). Together they provide the data needed to understand system behaviour and diagnose issues. A: Alerts and dashboards are tooling built on top of metrics, not separate pillars. B: Profiles (continuous profiling) are sometimes considered a fourth pillar but are not one of the canonical three. D: Spans, events, and counters are components within individual pillars.","doc_link":"https://opentelemetry.io/docs/concepts/observability-primer/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-285","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"A project joins the CNCF at the Sandbox level. What criteria must it demonstrate to graduate to the Incubating stage?","options":{"A":"It must have at least 1,000 GitHub stars and a paid support contract","B":"It must be used in production by at least three independent end users, have a healthy contributor base, and follow CNCF due diligence guidelines","C":"It must merge with another CNCF Sandbox project and pass a security audit","D":"It must achieve 100% test coverage and pass CNCF conformance tests"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: To move from Sandbox to Incubating, a project must document production usage by at least three independent end users, demonstrate a healthy number of committers from multiple organisations, show a substantial ongoing flow of commits and merged contributions, and complete the CNCF due diligence review. A: GitHub stars are not an official criterion and a paid contract is not required. C: Merging with another project is not a requirement. D: Test coverage thresholds and conformance tests are not Incubating criteria.","doc_link":"https://cncf.io/projects/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-286","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"The Container Runtime Interface (CRI) was introduced in Kubernetes to standardise how the kubelet communicates with container runtimes. Which of the following is a correct statement about CRI?","options":{"A":"CRI is a REST API that the API server uses to schedule pods onto nodes","B":"CRI is a gRPC API that allows the kubelet to work with any compliant runtime such as containerd or CRI-O without code changes","C":"CRI replaces the need for a container runtime entirely by running containers directly in the kernel","D":"CRI is a CNI plugin that manages pod networking on each node"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: CRI defines a gRPC interface (RuntimeService and ImageService) that the kubelet calls to manage container and image lifecycles. Any runtime that implements CRI — such as containerd or CRI-O — can be used without modifying the kubelet source code. A: CRI is used by the kubelet on the node, not by the API server for scheduling. C: CRI is an interface, not a kernel mechanism; a container runtime is still required. D: CNI (Container Network Interface) handles networking; CRI handles container execution.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/cri/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-287","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"hard","question":"A service mesh like Istio provides mTLS between services without requiring application code changes. Which component is responsible for enforcing this encryption?","options":{"A":"The Kubernetes API server, which terminates TLS for all pod-to-pod traffic","B":"The data-plane proxy (e.g., Envoy) injected as a sidecar into each pod","C":"The CNI plugin, which encrypts packets at the network layer","D":"The CoreDNS server, which issues certificates to each service"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: In a service mesh, the data-plane proxy (typically Envoy) is injected as a sidecar into every pod. The proxy intercepts all inbound and outbound traffic and enforces mTLS using certificates provisioned by the control plane (e.g., Istio's istiod). The application itself is unaware of the encryption. A: The API server handles Kubernetes control-plane traffic, not pod-to-pod data-plane traffic. C: CNI plugins manage pod networking at layer 3/4; mTLS enforcement is a layer 7 concern handled by the proxy. D: CoreDNS provides DNS resolution; certificate issuance is the role of the mesh control plane (e.g., istiod).","doc_link":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/security/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-288","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"What is the primary purpose of the Kubernetes Operator pattern?","options":{"A":"To replace the kube-scheduler with a custom scheduling algorithm","B":"To extend Kubernetes with domain-specific operational knowledge by combining a CRD with a custom controller that reconciles desired state","C":"To provide a GUI for managing Kubernetes clusters without using kubectl","D":"To package Helm charts with pre-built RBAC rules for multi-tenant clusters"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: An Operator encodes human operational knowledge into software. It pairs a Custom Resource Definition (CRD), which defines a new API type, with a custom controller that watches instances of that resource and reconciles the actual cluster state toward the desired state declared by the user. This enables automated management of stateful applications (e.g., databases) through Kubernetes-native APIs. A: Operators do not replace the scheduler; they add application-specific controllers. C: Operators are controller processes, not GUIs. D: While Operators may use Helm internally, their defining characteristic is the CRD + controller reconciliation loop.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/operator/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-289","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"easy","question":"According to the 12-factor app methodology, how should an application handle its configuration (factor III)?","options":{"A":"Store configuration in constants defined in the source code for reproducibility","B":"Store configuration in environment variables so it can vary between deploys without code changes","C":"Store configuration in a YAML file committed to the same repository as the code","D":"Store configuration in a database table that the application queries at startup"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: Factor III of the 12-factor methodology states that configuration — anything that varies between deploys (staging, production, etc.) — must be stored in environment variables. This strict separation of config from code ensures the same codebase can be deployed to any environment without modification. A: Hardcoding configuration violates the factor because it requires code changes to change configuration. C: Committing config files to the repo risks secrets exposure and couples config to a specific deploy. D: A database is not the recommended mechanism; environment variables are preferred for their simplicity and portability.","doc_link":"https://12factor.net/config","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-290","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"How does the CNCF define 'cloud native' in its official definition?","options":{"A":"Applications that run exclusively on public cloud providers such as AWS, GCP, or Azure","B":"Techniques that use containers, service meshes, microservices, immutable infrastructure, and declarative APIs to enable loosely coupled, resilient, manageable, and observable systems","C":"Any application written in a cloud-native language such as Go or Rust","D":"Workloads that rely on serverless functions and avoid the use of containers"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: The CNCF definition states that cloud-native technologies empower organisations to build and run scalable applications using techniques such as containers, service meshes, microservices, immutable infrastructure, and declarative APIs. These systems are loosely coupled, resilient, manageable, and observable. A: Cloud-native does not require public cloud; it can run on-premises or in hybrid environments. C: Language choice is not part of the cloud-native definition. D: Serverless is one cloud-native pattern but avoiding containers entirely contradicts the CNCF definition.","doc_link":"https://github.com/cncf/toc/blob/main/DEFINITION.md","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-291","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"What is the immutable infrastructure principle and why is it preferred in cloud-native deployments?","options":{"A":"Servers are patched in place using configuration management tools to apply updates without downtime","B":"Container images and server instances are never modified after deployment; updates are made by replacing them with new versions, ensuring consistency and eliminating configuration drift","C":"Infrastructure is defined in spreadsheets that are immutable once approved, preventing unauthorised changes","D":"Only the cluster control plane is immutable; worker nodes can be modified freely"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: Immutable infrastructure means that once a component (container image, VM) is deployed it is never modified. When a change is needed, a new image is built and the old instance is replaced. This eliminates configuration drift (where servers diverge over time due to ad-hoc changes) and makes rollbacks and audits straightforward. A: In-place patching is the mutable infrastructure model that immutable infrastructure replaces. C: Immutability refers to deployed artefacts, not documentation. D: The principle applies to all infrastructure components, not just the control plane.","doc_link":"https://cncf.io/blog/2021/01/13/immutable-infrastructure/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-292","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"hard","question":"How does the sidecar pattern differ from the Operator pattern in Kubernetes?","options":{"A":"A sidecar runs as a separate container inside the same pod as the main application to provide auxiliary capabilities; an Operator is a cluster-level controller that manages the lifecycle of complex applications using CRDs","B":"A sidecar manages cluster-wide resources using a CRD; an Operator injects itself into individual pods","C":"A sidecar and an Operator are functionally identical but differ only in their deployment method","D":"A sidecar replaces the main container during upgrades; an Operator handles pod scheduling"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Answer A: The sidecar pattern places a helper container in the same pod as the primary application container. The two share the pod's network namespace and volumes, enabling concerns like logging, proxying, or metrics export to be handled separately from application logic. An Operator, by contrast, is a Kubernetes controller process (usually deployed as a Deployment) that watches Custom Resources and reconciles the state of entire application stacks — it operates at the cluster level, not inside a single pod. B: This reverses the two patterns' responsibilities. C: They serve fundamentally different purposes at different scopes. D: Neither of these descriptions is accurate for either pattern.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/#using-pods","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-293","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"hard","question":"KEDA (Kubernetes Event-Driven Autoscaler) extends Kubernetes autoscaling. What is the key capability KEDA adds that the built-in HorizontalPodAutoscaler (HPA) cannot provide on its own?","options":{"A":"KEDA replaces the HPA and uses node CPU thresholds to scale pods faster than the default HPA","B":"KEDA allows pods to be scaled based on external event sources such as queue depth in Kafka, Azure Service Bus, or RabbitMQ, and can scale deployments to zero replicas","C":"KEDA provides vertical pod autoscaling by adjusting CPU and memory resource requests automatically","D":"KEDA monitors Prometheus metrics and alerts Alertmanager when scaling thresholds are breached"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: KEDA is a CNCF project that sits alongside the HPA and feeds it external metric data through the Kubernetes Metrics API. It supports dozens of scalers (Kafka, RabbitMQ, AWS SQS, Azure Service Bus, etc.) and crucially allows scaling a Deployment to zero replicas when there is no work, then scaling back up when events arrive — something the standard HPA cannot do. A: KEDA complements, not replaces, the HPA; it does not use only node CPU. C: Vertical scaling of resource requests is the role of the Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA), not KEDA. D: KEDA drives scaling actions directly; it does not route alerts through Alertmanager.","doc_link":"https://keda.sh/docs/latest/concepts/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-294","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"What is a Kubernetes Special Interest Group (SIG) and what is its purpose?","options":{"A":"A SIG is a team within a cloud provider responsible for the managed Kubernetes offering","B":"A SIG is a self-governing group of contributors who own and maintain a specific area of the Kubernetes project, such as networking, storage, or scheduling","C":"A SIG is a paid membership tier that gives organisations priority in the Kubernetes release process","D":"A SIG is a temporary task force created to respond to security vulnerabilities"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: Kubernetes Special Interest Groups (SIGs) are the primary organisational units of the Kubernetes open-source project. Each SIG owns a specific area (e.g., SIG Network, SIG Storage, SIG Auth) and is responsible for its design, implementation, and documentation. SIGs operate transparently with public meetings, mailing lists, and Slack channels. A: SIGs are community bodies, not cloud provider teams. C: SIG membership is open to any contributor; there is no paid tier. D: Security response is handled by the Kubernetes Security Response Committee, not ad-hoc SIGs.","doc_link":"https://github.com/kubernetes/community/blob/master/governance.md#sigs","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-295","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"easy","question":"What is the role of the CNCF Technical Oversight Committee (TOC)?","options":{"A":"The TOC writes the Kubernetes source code and merges pull requests","B":"The TOC approves the acceptance and graduation of projects within the CNCF, sets technical vision, and maintains the project maturity process","C":"The TOC manages CNCF finances and approves vendor sponsorships","D":"The TOC schedules KubeCon events and selects keynote speakers"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: The CNCF Technical Oversight Committee (TOC) is responsible for defining the technical vision for the CNCF, approving new projects (Sandbox), and reviewing projects for promotion to Incubating and Graduated status. It also creates and manages technical bodies such as Technical Advisory Groups (TAGs). A: The TOC does not write code for projects; that is done by project maintainers. C: Financial governance is the role of the CNCF Governing Board. D: Event logistics are handled by CNCF staff, not the TOC.","doc_link":"https://github.com/cncf/toc","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-296","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"easy","question":"How frequently does the Kubernetes project release new minor versions?","options":{"A":"Every month","B":"Every six months","C":"Approximately every four months (three releases per year)","D":"Once per year"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Answer C: The Kubernetes project follows a roughly four-month release cadence, producing approximately three minor releases per year (e.g., 1.28, 1.29, 1.30 across a calendar year). Each minor release is supported with patch releases for about fourteen months. A: Monthly releases would be unsustainable for a project of this complexity. B: Six-month cadence would yield only two releases per year, which is less frequent than Kubernetes actually ships. D: Annual releases would not keep pace with the ecosystem's rate of change.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/releases/release/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-297","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"medium","question":"What is a Kubernetes Enhancement Proposal (KEP) and when is one required?","options":{"A":"A KEP is a GitHub issue used to report bugs in the Kubernetes core components","B":"A KEP is a design document required for any significant change to Kubernetes — such as new API types, new features, or changes that affect stability — providing design rationale, graduation criteria, and a testing plan","C":"A KEP is a pull request template that must be filled in for all code contributions regardless of size","D":"A KEP is a security advisory published when a CVE is discovered in Kubernetes"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: A Kubernetes Enhancement Proposal (KEP) is a structured design document modelled on Python PEPs and Rust RFCs. It is required for any change that is large, user-facing, or affects API stability. A KEP must include motivation, design details, graduation criteria (Alpha to Beta to GA), and a testing plan. It is reviewed and approved by the relevant SIG before implementation begins. A: Bug reports use GitHub issues; KEPs are for enhancements. C: Small fixes and refactors do not require KEPs. D: Security advisories are published separately through the Kubernetes Security Response process.","doc_link":"https://github.com/kubernetes/enhancements/tree/master/keps","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-298","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"hard","question":"The Kubernetes API deprecation policy guarantees that beta APIs will remain available for a minimum period after deprecation. What is that guarantee for REST API versions in beta?","options":{"A":"Beta APIs are removed in the next minor release after deprecation with no grace period","B":"Beta APIs must be supported for at least 9 months or 3 minor releases (whichever is longer) after deprecation","C":"Beta APIs are supported indefinitely and are never removed","D":"Beta APIs are removed after 30 days regardless of the release cadence"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Answer B: The Kubernetes API deprecation policy states that beta API versions must be supported for a minimum of 9 months or 3 minor releases after the deprecation announcement, whichever is longer. This gives users and tooling authors time to migrate to stable (GA) API versions. A: Immediate removal would break users without warning and violates the policy. C: Beta APIs are intentionally transient; they move to GA or are removed. D: Calendar-based removal is not how Kubernetes API policy works; it is tied to release cycles.","doc_link":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/using-api/deprecation-policy/","llm_validated":0},
  {"id":"kcna-299","domain":"Cloud Native Architecture","difficulty":"hard","question":"A PromQL instant query uses a range vector selector such as http_errors_total[10m] inside rate(). What distinguishes an instant query from a range query in the Prometheus HTTP API?","options":{"A":"An instant query evaluates an expression at a single point in time and returns one value per time series; a range query evaluates the expression at multiple points across a time range and returns a matrix of values","B":"An instant query operates only on Gauges; a range query operates only on Counters","C":"An instant query runs in real time; a range query is always pre-computed and cached","D":"An instant query uses PromQL; a range query uses SQL syntax"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Answer A: The Prometheus HTTP API exposes /api/v1/query for instant queries (evaluated at a single timestamp, returning a vector) and /api/v1/query_range for range queries (evaluated at every step within a time window, returning a matrix). Both accept any PromQL expression. B: Both metric types can be used in both query types. C: Neither is necessarily pre-computed; both execute against the TSDB at query time. D: Both use PromQL; Prometheus does not have a SQL interface.","doc_link":"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/querying/api/","llm_validated":0},
